# Monorepo-level environment. Per-app values live in each app's own template
# (apps/<name>/.env.example); this file is read by ONE consumer, Compose.
#
# Copy to `.env` at the repo root — not `.env.local`, which Compose does not
# read. Every key below already has a working default inside
# docker-compose.yml, so `docker compose up -d` needs no root `.env` at all.
#
# Two line forms carry meaning here, the same convention the other templates
# use:
#
#   KEY=              unset.
#   # KEY=value       the default docker-compose.yml already applies as
#                     `${KEY:-value}`. Uncomment ONLY to override — a copied
#                     default is a second place to keep in step.

# ── Docker Compose overrides ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres reads user and database name only when it initialises an EMPTY data
# directory, so changing them against an existing volume silently does nothing
# and the healthcheck then fails. Run `docker compose down -v` first.
# POSTGRES_USER=chatofy
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=chatofy
# POSTGRES_DB=chatofy

# The API's own connection string is NOT read here — Compose does not use it.
# Set DATABASE_URL in apps/api/.env (see apps/api/.env.example) and keep it in
# step with the three values above.

# Host ports the dev stack binds on 127.0.0.1. The defaults are what every doc
# and template assumes, so leave them alone for a normal checkout.
#
# Change them when a SECOND checkout has to run at the same time — a git
# worktree. Compose scopes containers and volumes by project name, which
# defaults to the directory name, so a worktree is already isolated there; the
# host ports are the one resource both stacks would fight over. Give the
# worktree its own values here and mirror them in that worktree's
# apps/api/.env (DATABASE_URL, LOCAL_STT_URL, LOCAL_TTS_URL), or the API will
# talk to the other stack.
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# REDIS_PORT=6379
# LOCAL_STT_PORT=8002
# LOCAL_TTS_PORT=8003

# The two host apps do NOT read this file — pnpm and turbo never load a .env,
# only Compose does. api takes PORT from apps/api/.env; web takes WEB_PORT from
# the shell, so a worktree exports it or prefixes the command:
#   WEB_PORT=3003 pnpm dev

# CPU threads per speech engine in the sidecar containers. 8 (the physical core
# count on the machine these were measured on) beat 16 hyperthreads; lower it on
# a smaller host. docker-compose.prod.yml has its own PROD_-prefixed pair, so
# the two stacks can size differently on one machine.
# LOCAL_STT_THREADS=8
# LOCAL_TTS_THREADS=8

# How many turns the STT sidecar decodes at once. Raising it past the core
# count trades latency per turn for throughput across turns.
# LOCAL_STT_CONCURRENCY=4

# ── Model downloads (not read by the app or by Compose) ──────────────────────
# Hugging Face token for pulling gated models and benchmark corpora by hand.
# Nothing loads this file automatically, so the download and evaluation scripts
# take it from the shell:
#   export $(grep -v '^#' .env | xargs)   # or just export HF_TOKEN=...
HF_TOKEN=

# AI provider keys for the translate pipeline are NOT here — they belong to the
# api (ELEVENLABS_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY in apps/api/.env.example).
